::: {.cell-output-display}
![Sequential changes in alignment and classification on identical observations. A: primary extreme-group exposure contrasts with 95% simultaneous bands. B: daylength moderation from −1 to +1 hour of centered photoperiod, with 95% pointwise bands. Grey areas mark the original pre- and post-MSFsc comparison windows; zero marks the midsleep measure used in each scenario.](sensitivity/diary-msfsc/comparison/fixed_groups.png){#fig-dmsfsc-fixed-groups fig-alt='Two panels compare questionnaire timing and groups, diary timing with questionnaire groups retained, and diary timing and groups. The upper panel shows exposure ratios and the lower panel shows longer-day to shorter-day moderation ratios. All three use identical light observations.' width=810}
:::
:::


Both fixed-group fits converge without a basis-dimension flag. Standardized residual lag-one correlation is approximately 0.083, with residual spread similar to the other matched fits. Both exposure contrasts retain their directions with questionnaire membership fixed: 0.29 (95% CI 0.11–0.76) pre-MSFsc and 8.7 (3.5–21.7) post-MSFsc.

Changing alignment alone gives a post-MSFsc daylength factor of **2.39 (95% CI 0.95–6.02)**, compared with 2.66 (1.10–6.43) under matched questionnaire timing. The point estimate remains similar, but its interval now includes one. Also changing the quartile classification reduces the estimate to 1.15 (0.52–2.54). Thus reclassification produces the larger reduction in the estimated association in this sequence, while alignment alone is sufficient for the interval to cross the no-moderation reference. Pre-MSFsc daylength moderation remains unresolved in all three scenarios.

The [complete target comparison](sensitivity/diary-msfsc/comparison/fixed_groups_daylength_intervals.csv) also contains the quartile and 10th/90th-percentile targets. The comparisons isolate successive changes in the analysis specification; they do not constitute a formal test of the difference between the fitted moderation estimates.


